{
  "narrative_body": "Ten days before Andalusia votes, Pedro Sánchez personally awarded Spain's civil merit decoration to Francesca Albanese — the UN Special Rapporteur on Palestine, declared persona non grata by Israel and opposed by the current American administration — in a ceremony at the Moncloa complex staged, by all evidence, to be seen.\n\nThe decoration is a sovereign act, not a ministerial statement or a press release. On the same day, José Manuel Albares, the foreign minister, appeared before the full Congress to explain Spain's protection of the Gaza-bound Global Sumud Flotilla. Together the two moves confirm a shift: Spain's Gaza confrontation has moved from reactive defence to active sovereign honor, and it is being deployed as a domestic mobilization tool in the final stretch of a regional campaign. Notably, no White House or Israeli response materialized within the analysis window — which either means the confrontation is plateauing or the retaliation is simply pending.\n\nThat same week, King Felipe VI received 35 European navy chiefs at the Royal Palace for the CHENS-26 forum, where Margarita Robles, the defence minister, sat on a panel about contested maritime spaces and their consequences. The timing illustrates the posture Spain has settled into: selective friction with Washington and Tel Aviv, active embedding in European security architecture. The two are not in contradiction.\n\nThe domestic backdrop to all of this is the Andalusia election on May 17, which is drawing into view as one of the most consequential regional votes in years. The final GAD3 poll placed the PP at 52 seats — on the edge of an absolute majority without Vox. El Confidencial's analysis concluded that the PSOE had stopped trying to win and was managing for damage limitation, heading toward its worst result in the region's democratic history. Alberto Núñez Feijóo, the PP leader, used his central rally to frame the vote as \"Moreno and Andalusia against the criminal network Sánchez led and Montero supervised.\" The hantavirus crisis and the deaths of two Guardia Civil officers fed directly into that framing.\n\nThe hantavirus crisis began when Spain accepted a formal WHO request on May 5 to manage the repatriation of more than 140 passengers from the Dutch cruise ship MV Hondius, which had suffered an outbreak that killed three people. Sánchez met WHO Director-General Tedros Adhanom Ghebreyesus at the Moncloa before both traveled to Tenerife. The operation was completed, but not quietly. Fernando Clavijo, the PP president of the Canary Islands, refused to authorize late-night berthing at the port the central government had chosen, prompting government sources to accuse him of disloyalty; Mr Clavijo called Sánchez \"colonialist.\" Santiago Abascal, Vox's leader, told a live interview — within 15 seconds, by documented account — that the hantavirus had been \"invented by Sánchez to cover up corruption.\" Fact-checkers documented the claim. It spread regardless.\n\nThe two Guardia Civil officers had died on May 9 pursuing a drug-trafficking speedboat off the Huelva coast, the second such fatal incident on Spain's southern narcotics corridor since February 2024. Neither Sánchez nor Fernando Grande-Marlaska, the interior minister, attended the funeral. Sánchez offered condolences the following day at a PSOE campaign rally. Feijóo demanded Mr Marlaska's resignation and committed to restoring OCON-Sur, a dissolved narcotics coordination unit, if he governs.\n\nAmid all this, the Koldo corruption case widened further. In Supreme Court closing arguments, Víctor de Aldama's defense counsel announced that Mr Aldama holds additional material implicating PSOE figures including Francina Armengol, the president of Congress, along with former regional presidents of the Canary Islands and the Balearics. The anti-corruption prosecutor formally asked the Audiencia Nacional to incorporate testimony from Mr Aldama and Koldo García into its live investigation into PSOE financing through irregular contracts and cash payments. What began as a Transport Ministry scandal has reached the second-highest legislative office in the country.\n\nMay 17 will settle several things at once. If Juanma Moreno Bonilla wins an absolute majority without needing Vox, it validates a template that PP strategists have been watching for national application: a governing majority with Vox frozen out. If he falls short, the picture becomes considerably more complicated — for Andalusia, and for the party's calculation about what a post-Sánchez Spain might look like.",
